1
00:00:01,440 --> 00:00:06,400
In this video, we are going to look at 
another synchronization primitive: semaphores. 

2
00:00:07,120 --> 00:00:12,240
A semaphore is a special variable/data 
object that can be used for signaling. 

3
00:00:12,880 --> 00:00:16,960
It is handled via 2 atomic 
operations: wait and signal.  

4
00:00:17,600 --> 00:00:22,640
If a thread is waiting for a signal, it will 
remain blocked until that signal is sent. 

5
00:00:23,520 --> 00:00:28,640
We have seen such a behavior in the lecture 
about multi-threaded processes and pthreads. 

6
00:00:29,600 --> 00:00:33,200
Here we have the description 
of how signal and wait work. 

7
00:00:34,400 --> 00:00:40,240
When invoking signal on variable S, 
the variable is atomically incremented. 

8
00:00:40,240 --> 00:00:44,800
When invoking wait on S, the thread 
invoking the function will loop or  

9
00:00:44,800 --> 00:00:51,920
remain blocked until S value is 0. After that, 
it will exit after decreasing the value of S. 

10
00:00:53,600 --> 00:00:58,000
S can take only values 0 and 
1 if it is a binary semaphore  

11
00:00:58,000 --> 00:01:00,960
or also greater than one if 
it is a counting semaphore.

12
00:01:03,280 --> 00:01:08,880
At this point, you can hopefully see semaphores 
can be used as shown in this pseudocode to  

13
00:01:08,880 --> 00:01:13,280
handle the critical section problem (but 
please try to reason formally by yourself!)

14
00:01:14,960 --> 00:01:20,480
Something worth noticing about semaphores 
is that they can be used to create an order  

15
00:01:20,480 --> 00:01:27,520
in the execution of two threads. If we 
want to execute code segment opB from TB  

16
00:01:27,520 --> 00:01:35,600
only after code segment opA has been executed 
in Ta, we can do it as shown in the slide. Since  

17
00:01:35,600 --> 00:01:45,520
B waits for a signal from A, and A sends a signal 
after opA, then opB will be executed after opA.

